This directory is a set of tasks to classify the most relevant division for 
a pubication based on its title and journal.   


Description
-----------

Divisions for each publication were identified using a machine learning model
that was based on a data set that had identified the original funding source for a
set of representative publications.

The data set matched publications from 2024 to the funding division through an
internal data set of grant identification numbers and division funding. This
included 1986 publications that were successfully matched to a single division.
The data set was split into an independent training (80%) and testing (20%) data
sets.
A simple two parameter model was then built to predict the division based on the
input title and journal name of the publication. The journal titles were each
treated as a unique entry, and a dictionary was built with the number of divisions
funding a publication appearing in that journal. Titles were split into individual,
lower case words with non-alphabet characters removed. For each word, a
dictionary was built with the number of divisions funding a publication with a title
that uses that word. These dictionaries were then converted into distributions by
normalizing by the total number of publications using that journal or word.

Based on the journal name and title, the model would return a probability for the
publication to be funded by each division. This would be calculated from
multiplying the probability calculated from the journal title and the probability
calculated from the title. After different attempts to optimize the weighting of the
probability from the two different measurements, it was found that the best
results were found by setting the probably to one when the journal title was only
associated with a single division.

With this model, the division of the publication was accurately predicted for 75%
of the training data set. As divisions do have overlapping subject matter such as
atmospheres and exoplanets, it is likely that this is close to the maximum
accuracy that could be expected, but further study would be needed. This is
likely sufficient classification for the purposes needed in this study that can be
further verified by the reviewer.

Programs
--------

run_publication_classifier.py: Given a title and a journal name, return the most likely division and a set of probabilities.  This requires two files to be included with the probabilities for words ('distribution_title_word.tsv')  or for journal names ('distribution_journal_name.tsv') 


train_publication_classifier.py: A script to create the predictive model using a training data set
test_publication_classifier.py: A script to test the predictive model using a test data set
division_from_usa_spending.py: This is script to use the USA Spending API to determine the relevant NASA division for an award given the grant ID
filter_data.py: Take the original data set, clean the data, and then create the test and training data sets

Files
-----
nasa_publications_division_spending.csv: File containing infomration about publications.  The last column includes the primary division that funded the publication.
distribution_journal_name.tsv: Given that an article has NASA funding, it is the probability that an article published in this journal has funding from a given division. 
distribution_title_word.tsv: Given that an article has NASA funding, it is the probability that an article with this word in the title has funding from a given division. 
